Let the Editor hold draco's innate ontology, guarded by Uniqueness and update-by-identity

Dev's decisions, 2026-10-12, one point at a time: the ontology being built is draco's
innate, endogenous ontology — the framework's own domains plus the dreams definitions as
they can be accepted, "highly self-referential, and we need to be careful how it is
maintained"; the Editor may hold the accepted ontology "as long as that acceptance
doesn't compromise the integrity of the framework itself"; Uniqueness is accepted OVER
NAME AND PACKAGE WITH PARAMETERS FREE (parameters compose references, not definitions);
update-by-identity is accepted (a domain of the same name and package is replaced).

draco.Uniqueness: a core rule joining `var m TypeDefinition` with `var o DomainOntology`,
a Problem for every definition whose name is owned by more than one composed domain.
Its home is core because identity is framework meaning, not editing workflow; the Draco
actor's knowledge gains it as a member rule.

DomainOntology.including now REPLACES a composed domain of the same name and package
instead of adding beside it. A resubmitted domain is an update; Uniqueness then judges
the replacement like any creation.

Editor: `mut accepted` starts as the ontology it was minted with; per Latent it builds
accepted.including(candidate), inserts EVERY composed domain and EVERY definition of that
provisional ontology plus the ontology itself, fires the five core rules in a fresh
session, and swaps the state through
guard(problems.isEmpty).ifThenElse(accept(provisional), accept(accepted)) — only when
every rule is quiet. Validating the whole resulting ontology, not just the candidate,
is what makes an update safe: dropping a member another domain derives from is refused,
naming the dependent. Acceptance generates and activates nothing; the Actual stays the
report on one domain; delivering the grown ontology to a caller waits for the
initialization client.

